1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Meat, heat, and mouth-watering intense flavors.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
An old-fashioned style of cooking has evolved into a modern food craze

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:12,000
and spawned a multi-billion dollar industry.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:17,000
From torrents of briquettes to extravagant backyard cooking environments.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Now, BBQ Tech on Modern Marvelous.

6
00:00:40,294 --> 00:00:51,136
Almost any weekend, clouds of smoke can be seen rising from a park or parking lot somewhere in the United States in what has become an American tradition, the barbecue

7
00:00:51,137 --> 00:00:51,837
cook-off.

8
00:00:51,838 --> 00:01:04,140
Competitors haul in all manner of cooking contraptions, from simple smokers to custom-built steel behemoths that look like Jules Verne had a hand in designing them.

9
00:01:05,540 --> 00:01:10,800
Eye-catching team names and equipment, along with a laid-back party atmosphere, help draw in the public.

10
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:16,240
Their reward? The smells and taste thrills of real barbecue.

11
00:01:17,460 --> 00:01:19,720
There's something primal about barbecue.

12
00:01:19,721 --> 00:01:26,460
When you smell that smoke and then you get a bite of that meat, it just hits a primal button in you.

13
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:31,320
Despite the fun and good food, cook-offs are serious business to the competitors.

14
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:38,740
Recently, the Autry Museum of the American West in Los Angeles held its first annual cook-off.

15
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:41,520
And there was more at stake than just prize money.

16
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:48,460
Los Angeles is just now getting into the market or the venue of barbecue cook-offs.

17
00:01:48,461 --> 00:01:51,260
You have all the big-name competitors out here today.

18
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:54,660
They all want to win because we all want to be able to walk away

19
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:59,900
and say we were the first winner of the Autry cook-off.

20
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,400
Each team brings the size and shape of equipment

21
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:04,300
it feels will turn out the most savory,

22
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:07,320
lip-smacking product possible.

23
00:02:07,420 --> 00:02:10,720
This is what I consider an L-shaped smoker.

24
00:02:10,820 --> 00:02:16,660
There's a firebox down here, and there's four chambers.

25
00:02:16,661 --> 00:02:21,060
The reason I like this is because I get a great variance of temperature

26
00:02:21,061 --> 00:02:25,660
from over here at 300 degrees down to over here at 150 degrees.

27
00:02:25,661 --> 00:02:29,300
So I can control the amount of time I cook certain meats.

28
00:02:29,301 --> 00:02:33,580
This is a barbecue. It's not unusual in its design or concept.

29
00:02:33,581 --> 00:02:35,340
It's out there on the market.

30
00:02:35,341 --> 00:02:39,820
What we did is taken and added all the stainless and diamond plate

31
00:02:39,821 --> 00:02:43,020
and, you know, carved flames into the stacks.

32
00:02:43,021 --> 00:02:46,820
by the way, that some truckers are going to be really pissed off someday when he wakes up

33
00:02:46,821 --> 00:02:49,780
and finds his stacks gone, but that's another story.

34
00:02:50,520 --> 00:02:53,220
Today I'm cooking on the Weber Smoky Mountain,

35
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:58,200
which is a retail piece of equipment that's available to the homeowner,

36
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:02,320
and it's something that we use a lot in the barbecue circuit.

37
00:03:03,380 --> 00:03:06,520
When it comes to the judging, the judging's blind,

38
00:03:06,521 --> 00:03:11,880
so the judges don't know whether it come off the $200 pit or the $20,000 pit.

39
00:03:11,881 --> 00:03:13,520
They bring their entries in.

40
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:15,900
We actually change the numbers,

41
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:20,540
so there's no chance that the judges will know whose entry they're judging.

42
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:27,020
And they judge that on appearance, taste, and tenderness.

43
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:30,320
Not all cook-offs are run the same.

44
00:03:31,140 --> 00:03:34,620
In South Carolina, the tradition is to cook whole hogs,

45
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:36,940
which can take up to 20 hours or more.

46
00:03:36,941 --> 00:03:43,660
To keep things fair, the hogs are supplied to the contestants the day the cook-off begins,

47
00:03:43,661 --> 00:03:46,720
so no preparations can be made in advance.

48
00:03:48,040 --> 00:03:48,880
Thank you, sir.

49
00:03:49,880 --> 00:03:52,840
Cooking whole hog really separates the men from the boys.

50
00:03:53,020 --> 00:03:54,000
It is tough.

51
00:03:55,240 --> 00:04:00,680
Anybody can cook some ribs, even I can cook ribs, but it takes an artist to cook whole hog.

52
00:04:00,681 --> 00:04:03,680
Many contestants come armed with units custom-built

53
00:04:03,681 --> 00:04:07,680
to tackle the task of cooking a really big pig.

54
00:04:07,681 --> 00:04:11,680
They've also got a practical knowledge of physics.

55
00:04:11,681 --> 00:04:15,680
How this thing works is it's fired from the bottom,

56
00:04:15,681 --> 00:04:17,680
and we burn wood.

57
00:04:17,681 --> 00:04:19,680
We burn either peach or wild cherry.

58
00:04:19,681 --> 00:04:22,680
It's an indirect cooker, meaning the flames come up,

59
00:04:22,681 --> 00:04:24,680
hit the bottom of the water pan, heat the water up

60
00:04:24,681 --> 00:04:27,680
so you've got a mild, moist, gentle steam rising from the bottom.

61
00:04:27,681 --> 00:04:29,680
Then you've got the dry heat that comes up around the center

62
00:04:29,681 --> 00:04:33,520
heat that comes up around the sides of the pig and then drafts back out the top.

63
00:04:33,521 --> 00:04:35,720
What that does, it rolls inside.

64
00:04:35,721 --> 00:04:39,620
You've got dry heat meeting moist heat, so therefore you've got a natural convection.

65
00:04:39,621 --> 00:04:45,240
It gives you a real mild, real tender, very smoky flavor to your meat.

66
00:04:45,241 --> 00:04:49,760
To the diehards on the cook-off circuit, the term barbecue is often misused by the general

67
00:04:49,761 --> 00:04:50,760
public.

68
00:04:50,761 --> 00:04:56,620
One of the most important things for people to understand is the difference between barbecuing

69
00:04:56,621 --> 00:04:57,620
and grilling.

70
00:04:57,621 --> 00:04:58,620
And there is a big difference.

71
00:04:58,621 --> 00:05:03,840
With grilling, you're cooking hot and fast, searing the meat over the direct influence of the flame,

72
00:05:04,080 --> 00:05:06,740
and you're cooking at temperatures of 400-plus degrees.

73
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:09,160
Now, barbecuing is the exact opposite.

74
00:05:09,161 --> 00:05:13,980
You're cooking low and slow, 180 to 225 degrees,

75
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:17,740
indirectly away from the flame and away from the heat source.

76
00:05:18,820 --> 00:05:25,060
The origins of this low and slow culinary art have been obscured in a smoky mixture of history and folklore.

77
00:05:25,061 --> 00:05:33,120
I always heard that over in China, a house burnt down, I'm probably wrong, and had some

78
00:05:33,121 --> 00:05:37,580
pigs in it, and said they still went to tasting it, and that's how barbecue came around.

79
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:40,400
That might just be an old fairy tale, but I'm telling you what I heard.

80
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:48,420
Through the ages, everyone from cavemen to the kings of England has enjoyed a feast of

81
00:05:48,421 --> 00:05:51,080
wild game or livestock cooked over an open flame.

82
00:05:51,081 --> 00:05:54,720
But we can't really be sure of their cooking techniques

83
00:05:54,721 --> 00:05:55,880
qualified as barbecue.

84
00:05:58,580 --> 00:06:00,660
There's a greater degree of certainty, however,

85
00:06:00,780 --> 00:06:02,500
about the origin of the word itself.

86
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:06,920
When the Spanish were exploring the New World,

87
00:06:07,320 --> 00:06:09,620
they came across the native people of the West Indies

88
00:06:09,621 --> 00:06:11,720
cooking their meats over a smoky fire

89
00:06:11,721 --> 00:06:16,680
on a rack made of green saplings called a barbacot.

90
00:06:18,200 --> 00:06:20,680
The Spaniards came to call it barbacoa.

91
00:06:21,080 --> 00:06:26,040
Still, the smoked flavor wasn't so much the aim of the process as an accidental byproduct.

92
00:06:26,820 --> 00:06:31,940
The real purpose of the technique was to preserve the meat and simply keep the flies away.

93
00:06:33,440 --> 00:06:37,520
From here, the history of barbecue in North America splinters according to region.

94
00:06:38,700 --> 00:06:43,040
The Carolinas' tradition of cooking whole hog originates with their earliest settlements.

95
00:06:43,900 --> 00:06:47,660
The pig is not indigenous to the Western Hemisphere, to the Americas.

96
00:06:47,661 --> 00:06:51,520
And so the Europeans, the Spaniards, brought the pig with them.

97
00:06:52,400 --> 00:07:00,040
And the first colony, the first colony of any European nation was in South Carolina.

98
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:04,440
And there their Indian friends were, the Native Americans,

99
00:07:04,820 --> 00:07:10,280
they are the people who actually had learned how to cook with gradation of heat.

100
00:07:10,980 --> 00:07:14,760
In the Southwest, barbecue evolved on the frontier cattle drives,

101
00:07:14,761 --> 00:07:19,180
where cowboys rode the range with a beef dinner walking right next to them.

102
00:07:21,300 --> 00:07:24,980
Today, Southwest Barbecue is still identified with the Cowboy Mystique.

103
00:07:26,540 --> 00:07:30,120
The decor in restaurants like the County Line in Houston, Texas,

104
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:32,940
reflects the region's Western heritage.

105
00:07:34,140 --> 00:07:35,940
But as far as the food is concerned,

106
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:40,560
cowboys never enjoyed meat barbecued in large wood-burning smokers

107
00:07:40,561 --> 00:07:42,380
with a heavy-duty rotisserie system.

108
00:07:42,381 --> 00:07:45,380
This is the heart of the restaurant,

109
00:07:45,381 --> 00:07:48,380
the smokers' oil circle pits.

110
00:07:48,381 --> 00:07:52,380
And you got a chain driven by a very slow speed

111
00:07:52,381 --> 00:07:56,380
but very high-torque motor, and it needs to be high-torque.

112
00:07:56,381 --> 00:07:58,380
It's got to have a lot of strength.

113
00:07:58,381 --> 00:08:01,380
You're going to end up with 160 racks of baby backs in there,

114
00:08:01,381 --> 00:08:03,380
perhaps 50 briskets.

115
00:08:03,381 --> 00:08:06,380
You're talking about maybe 1,000 pounds of meat.

116
00:08:06,381 --> 00:08:07,380
Then this pit is huge.

117
00:08:07,381 --> 00:08:10,380
It's a big piece of space in there,

118
00:08:10,381 --> 00:08:15,360
So you got to do the whole thing, cooking through it, back and forth, up and down on it.

119
00:08:15,700 --> 00:08:19,080
And that's what the chain and the rotisserie system achieved for us.

120
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:24,720
The cooking heat is determined by the amount of wood burning

121
00:08:24,721 --> 00:08:29,560
and a temperature control that operates dampers to adjust airflow as needed.

122
00:08:31,040 --> 00:08:33,660
175 is our cold smoking temperature.

123
00:08:34,500 --> 00:08:40,240
225 is more of the finishing up, so it's nice and hot for you to eat.

124
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:41,680
That's what they all be doing.

125
00:08:42,820 --> 00:08:43,520
Wow.

126
00:08:43,521 --> 00:08:44,221
Wow.

127
00:08:46,820 --> 00:08:50,440
During the heyday of the 19th century Texas cattle drives,

128
00:08:51,040 --> 00:08:54,540
the final destination for many of the herds was Kansas City.

129
00:08:55,980 --> 00:08:57,800
Since the early 20th century,

130
00:08:57,980 --> 00:09:00,400
Kansas City and barbecue have been almost synonymous.

131
00:09:01,700 --> 00:09:03,080
It didn't originate there,

132
00:09:03,500 --> 00:09:06,100
but it was where all the crucial elements came together.

133
00:09:07,700 --> 00:09:09,820
Kansas City became a barbecue mecca

134
00:09:09,821 --> 00:09:13,060
because, well, first of all, we had people with barbecue

135
00:09:13,061 --> 00:09:16,420
knowledge who migrated primarily from southern states.

136
00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:20,240
We also had lots of hickory and oak nearby.

137
00:09:21,020 --> 00:09:24,380
We also had the meat because we had huge stockyards.

138
00:09:25,080 --> 00:09:27,000
That's the combination you need.

139
00:09:27,160 --> 00:09:28,420
It's been a great marriage.

140
00:09:29,100 --> 00:09:31,920
Back then, barbecue was considered poor people's food

141
00:09:31,921 --> 00:09:35,380
because the process made even the cheapest, toughest cut of meat

142
00:09:35,381 --> 00:09:36,081
more edible.

143
00:09:36,082 --> 00:09:41,220
Back in the 20s, I mean, they even had wild animals.

144
00:09:41,221 --> 00:09:46,980
They was barbecuing coon, possum, or meat they'd destroyed from the packing house that

145
00:09:46,981 --> 00:09:48,820
the mainstream people didn't want.

146
00:09:48,821 --> 00:09:51,300
That's how it started here, Kansas City Barbecue.

147
00:09:52,900 --> 00:09:56,900
Gone are the days when barbecue's main purpose was to salvage unappetizing meat.

148
00:09:59,460 --> 00:10:05,060
Today, many aficionados make a pilgrimage to this Kansas City restaurant, Arthur Bryant's.

149
00:10:05,940 --> 00:10:10,940
Here they can truly sink their teeth into the city's early

150
00:10:10,941 --> 00:10:12,940
barbecue heritage.

151
00:10:12,941 --> 00:10:16,940
In the early 50s, Mr. Bryan relocated to here, 17th and

152
00:10:16,941 --> 00:10:17,940
Brooklyn.

153
00:10:17,941 --> 00:10:19,940
And this used to be an old bakery.

154
00:10:19,941 --> 00:10:20,940
This used to be the oven.

155
00:10:20,941 --> 00:10:24,940
And Mr. Bryan converted the oven into a pit.

156
00:10:24,941 --> 00:10:26,940
That's why you need a good pit man, because his temperature

157
00:10:26,941 --> 00:10:30,940
in this pit can get up to 500 degrees.

158
00:10:30,941 --> 00:10:32,340
because the temperature in this peak

159
00:10:32,341 --> 00:10:36,340
can get up to 500 degrees.

160
00:10:36,341 --> 00:10:37,720
But we try to keep our temperature

161
00:10:37,721 --> 00:10:41,060
between 150 and 300 in the process of cooking

162
00:10:41,061 --> 00:10:42,120
our briskets and ribs.

163
00:10:42,121 --> 00:10:46,060
Thank you, sir.

164
00:10:46,061 --> 00:10:50,300
Greater Kansas City boasts more than 90 barbecue restaurants.

165
00:10:50,301 --> 00:10:57,940
Some have gone upscale, while others are decidedly down home.

166
00:10:57,941 --> 00:10:59,660
Backyard grilling and barbecuing

167
00:10:59,661 --> 00:11:06,220
didn't become a great American pastime until shortly after World War II, when G.I.'s returned

168
00:11:06,221 --> 00:11:08,904
home and settled into suburbia.

169
00:11:08,905 --> 00:11:13,580
One of them, George Stephen, was destined to help lead

170
00:11:13,581 --> 00:11:14,281
the way.

171
00:11:14,680 --> 00:11:20,060
My father, like a lot of Americans coming back from World War II, was basically cooking

172
00:11:20,061 --> 00:11:23,235
on a brick structure just like this.

173
00:11:23,236 --> 00:11:27,820
And it would flare up, he couldn't control anything,

174
00:11:27,821 --> 00:11:31,500
So he never was very satisfied with the way that worked,

175
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,220
which got him thinking about he could do something better.

176
00:11:34,680 --> 00:11:38,520
That led him to inventing the original Weber kettle right here.

177
00:11:38,780 --> 00:11:43,580
Where the invention really lies with this is in the fact that putting the lid on

178
00:11:43,581 --> 00:11:48,580
really affected the way the heat was used in the barbecue and made it more efficient.

179
00:11:49,300 --> 00:11:54,500
And by putting dampers in it, he could control the amount of air going through the barbecue.

180
00:11:54,501 --> 00:11:58,960
so he could control the heat and the flare-up and the cooking process itself.

181
00:11:59,300 --> 00:12:02,160
George Stephen worked for the Weber Brothers Metalworks,

182
00:12:02,620 --> 00:12:05,620
and together they made the kettle a big success.

183
00:12:07,100 --> 00:12:10,360
It's gone through some variations and refinements over the years

184
00:12:10,361 --> 00:12:15,060
and is now manufactured in an enormous facility in Huntley, Illinois.

185
00:12:15,880 --> 00:12:20,200
This is the only factory in the world that makes the Weber charcoal grill,

186
00:12:20,201 --> 00:12:27,200
and behind me there is machinery making the different sized charcoal grills that we manufacture.

187
00:12:27,201 --> 00:12:37,200
We have 200 ton to 500 ton presses to press the different shapes of the bowls and lids.

188
00:12:37,201 --> 00:12:44,200
On the opposite side of the room are other presses that make the attaching parts for the grills themselves.

189
00:12:44,201 --> 00:12:48,860
for the grills themselves.

190
00:12:48,861 --> 00:12:53,100
The way the classic kettle is made, or any kettle is made,

191
00:12:53,101 --> 00:12:56,760
we put a steel blank in a drawing compound,

192
00:12:56,761 --> 00:13:00,180
which is like a soap material, to prevent the metal

193
00:13:00,181 --> 00:13:05,320
from wrinkling or cracking.

194
00:13:05,321 --> 00:13:08,360
What happens then is the press closes,

195
00:13:08,361 --> 00:13:12,200
and then a ram comes down through to actually form the shape.

196
00:13:14,200 --> 00:13:16,960
From there, the kettles move on to another press

197
00:13:16,961 --> 00:13:19,840
to have air holes punched and excess steel removed.

198
00:13:22,640 --> 00:13:24,520
After parts are attached, the kettles

199
00:13:24,521 --> 00:13:25,880
are hung on a conveyor system.

200
00:13:31,360 --> 00:13:33,160
First, they pass through a washer

201
00:13:33,161 --> 00:13:36,960
to remove the soap compound and any oil and residues,

202
00:13:36,961 --> 00:13:39,320
so they can be coated with a porcelain enamel finish.

203
00:13:39,321 --> 00:13:44,320
They go into our furnace, where they're fired at 1,600 degrees

204
00:13:44,321 --> 00:13:45,320
Fahrenheit.

205
00:13:45,321 --> 00:13:49,320
That gives them the durability and the shiny surface

206
00:13:49,321 --> 00:13:51,320
that you see on all Weber products.

207
00:13:54,320 --> 00:13:57,320
The coating on each kettle is quality checked in a room

208
00:13:57,321 --> 00:13:59,320
with lighting approximating daylight.

209
00:14:02,320 --> 00:14:06,320
Next, in the packaging area, handles and dampers

210
00:14:06,321 --> 00:14:08,320
are attached to the kettles.

211
00:14:08,321 --> 00:14:12,120
which then are packaged with all the other necessary parts

212
00:14:12,220 --> 00:14:14,560
for shipment to destinations around the world.

213
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:19,940
After more than 50 years, the Weber Kettle

214
00:14:20,040 --> 00:14:22,260
is still a big seller in what has become

215
00:14:22,360 --> 00:14:26,000
a very competitive marketplace, exploding

216
00:14:26,100 --> 00:14:29,140
with alternative designs and endless new options

217
00:14:29,240 --> 00:14:31,580
to choose from.

218
00:14:31,680 --> 00:14:34,940
The one burning question facing every backyard chef

219
00:14:35,040 --> 00:14:36,740
is which fuel to choose.

220
00:14:36,741 --> 00:14:41,740
Will it pour from a bag or come hissing out of a tank?

221
00:14:44,740 --> 00:14:47,580
Kansas City's American Royal Barbecue Contest

222
00:14:47,581 --> 00:14:49,580
is one of the largest cook-offs in the world.

223
00:14:49,581 --> 00:14:52,980
Many of the judges eat more than seven pounds of meat

224
00:14:52,981 --> 00:14:56,700
in just over 24 hours to determine the winner.

225
00:14:56,701 --> 00:14:59,700
Barbecue Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

226
00:14:59,701 --> 00:15:06,700
The original and the most basic barbecue fuel is wood,

227
00:15:06,701 --> 00:15:10,700
especially if the goal is to add a real smoked flavor.

228
00:15:10,701 --> 00:15:13,700
But logs, along with the jumbo equipment to burn them,

229
00:15:13,701 --> 00:15:16,700
aren't always practical for the home barbecuer.

230
00:15:16,701 --> 00:15:19,700
One alternative is the wood pellet.

231
00:15:19,701 --> 00:15:23,700
The Traeger pellet mill in Mount Angel, Oregon,

232
00:15:23,701 --> 00:15:26,700
begins its production process with sawdust

233
00:15:26,701 --> 00:15:28,700
from local furniture manufacturers.

234
00:15:28,701 --> 00:15:30,700
manufacturers

235
00:15:31,960 --> 00:15:37,480
Material is brought in from the top here comes down goes through what we call a conditioner that blends it together

236
00:15:37,481 --> 00:15:44,200
And it comes down and is dropped down into the die and we'll go ahead and open the door here so you can take a look

237
00:15:47,540 --> 00:15:53,020
And as you can see the material comes out here and drops down and goes in the center of the die

238
00:15:53,960 --> 00:15:57,880
Inside the die there's two rolls this die turns

239
00:15:58,700 --> 00:16:04,440
And the rolls force the material out through the die.

240
00:16:04,880 --> 00:16:07,240
This die gets up to about 400 degrees,

241
00:16:07,760 --> 00:16:09,900
and there's lignans that are in the wood,

242
00:16:10,180 --> 00:16:12,040
and the lignans turn to a liquid.

243
00:16:12,220 --> 00:16:13,140
It's like a glue.

244
00:16:13,740 --> 00:16:16,860
So when they come out, these pellets are about 400 degrees,

245
00:16:17,460 --> 00:16:18,800
and they're kind of soft.

246
00:16:19,480 --> 00:16:22,180
But the lignan, the glue, is what keeps them together.

247
00:16:23,760 --> 00:16:26,260
After being extruded and cut into pellets,

248
00:16:26,261 --> 00:16:29,260
the material is carried off on a conveyor to be cooled and bagged.

249
00:16:32,660 --> 00:16:35,600
A few miles away, in a 100-year-old former dairy

250
00:16:35,700 --> 00:16:38,000
building, Traeger Industries also produces

251
00:16:38,100 --> 00:16:39,200
its own line of grills.

252
00:16:42,500 --> 00:16:45,020
Inside each of them is an electrically powered system

253
00:16:45,120 --> 00:16:49,120
developed to efficiently burn pellets.

254
00:16:49,220 --> 00:16:53,220
The first component on it is a 1 and 1 half RPM motor

255
00:16:53,221 --> 00:16:59,060
that dries the auger, which feeds the pellets from the hopper

256
00:16:59,061 --> 00:17:02,360
down to the fire pot.

257
00:17:02,361 --> 00:17:05,760
On the bottom of the fire pot, there's an igniter.

258
00:17:05,761 --> 00:17:08,360
When the burner is turned on, the element heats up

259
00:17:08,361 --> 00:17:10,940
and then catches the pellets on fire.

260
00:17:10,941 --> 00:17:15,040
One pound of pellets outputs 8,500 BTUs

261
00:17:15,041 --> 00:17:17,780
and can last up to an hour or more.

262
00:17:17,781 --> 00:17:20,380
The ability to add more pellets to the hopper as needed

263
00:17:20,381 --> 00:17:23,140
makes this an efficient way to maintain consistent heat

264
00:17:23,240 --> 00:17:25,180
over the long process of real barbecue.

265
00:17:28,080 --> 00:17:31,820
A more traditional way to cook barbecue is with charcoal.

266
00:17:31,920 --> 00:17:33,720
Charcoal is produced by burning wood

267
00:17:33,820 --> 00:17:36,720
until water vapor and other materials are removed,

268
00:17:36,820 --> 00:17:40,140
reducing the wood to almost pure carbon.

269
00:17:40,240 --> 00:17:42,340
Workers outside of Jackie Heights restaurant

270
00:17:42,440 --> 00:17:45,180
in Leesville, South Carolina, use a firebox

271
00:17:45,280 --> 00:17:48,840
to produce a steady supply of coals.

272
00:17:48,841 --> 00:17:54,300
The firebox feeds heat to the restaurant's pits for the entire time the hogs are being cooked.

273
00:17:55,660 --> 00:17:59,620
This is the way you burn down real wood to get charcoal to put in your pits.

274
00:17:59,720 --> 00:18:02,420
It's real charcoal. It's not imitation.

275
00:18:03,980 --> 00:18:07,780
Workers carry shovel loads into the pits as needed to maintain the temperature.

276
00:18:08,700 --> 00:18:11,380
They're spread around the edges to create indirect heat.

277
00:18:13,080 --> 00:18:17,560
The cooking temperature is kept so low that cardboard can be used to cover the pits.

278
00:18:18,840 --> 00:18:22,840
You couldn't use cardboard if you get them any hotter than we do.

279
00:18:22,841 --> 00:18:24,840
We fire these hogs.

280
00:18:24,841 --> 00:18:27,840
In the wintertime, we have to go every 20 or 25 minutes.

281
00:18:27,841 --> 00:18:31,840
In the summer, usually every 30 minutes, if you put the right amount of coal to your shovel.

282
00:18:31,841 --> 00:18:35,840
While Jackie Height prefers to burn down his own coals,

283
00:18:35,841 --> 00:18:40,840
most of us let others do the hard work and buy them in a bag.

284
00:18:40,841 --> 00:18:43,840
When mass produced for commercial distribution,

285
00:18:43,841 --> 00:18:47,840
charcoal made from 100% wood is called lump.

286
00:18:47,841 --> 00:18:54,460
But for about 80 years, briquettes have been the most popular way to cook over coals, thanks

287
00:18:54,461 --> 00:18:57,200
to automaker Henry Ford.

288
00:18:57,201 --> 00:19:00,040
It started back in 1922.

289
00:19:00,041 --> 00:19:02,600
Henry Ford was the originator.

290
00:19:02,601 --> 00:19:08,460
He was looking for something to do with the excess wood he had from making the Model T.

291
00:19:08,461 --> 00:19:13,100
Ford learned that he could take this excess wood and make briquettes out of it.

292
00:19:13,101 --> 00:19:16,829
The original plant was designed by two prominent people, E.G.

293
00:19:16,830 --> 00:19:18,540
Kingsford, who is a relative

294
00:19:18,541 --> 00:19:23,700
of Henry Ford and who the brand is named after today, and Thomas Edison.

295
00:19:23,701 --> 00:19:31,300
Today, the Kingsford Company uses more than a million tons of waste wood each year.

296
00:19:31,301 --> 00:19:35,480
One of their plants, located in Springfield, Oregon, receives several daily truckloads

297
00:19:35,481 --> 00:19:40,160
of waste from local lumber mills.

298
00:19:40,161 --> 00:19:47,160
The constructs entire trailer is lifted until its contents are poured out and added to the enormous wood pile.

299
00:19:47,161 --> 00:19:55,160
After the product is dumped, we have a bulldozer where what we do is make it into a consistent pile.

300
00:19:55,161 --> 00:20:03,160
Since we get it from different mills in the area, the wood can be slightly different from mill to mill.

301
00:20:03,161 --> 00:20:09,160
This is what the material looks like as we get it in from the trucks and you can see the particle size in it.

302
00:20:09,161 --> 00:20:11,957
it. It also does have quite a bit of moisture to it.

303
00:20:11,958 --> 00:20:13,840
And we put this on the pile and age

304
00:20:13,841 --> 00:20:15,695
it for approximately six to eight weeks.

305
00:20:15,696 --> 00:20:17,920
It's very similar to what you might have at home

306
00:20:17,921 --> 00:20:19,209
as a compost pile.

307
00:20:19,210 --> 00:20:24,080
This material is actually being seasoned in the pile, and so it does

308
00:20:24,081 --> 00:20:27,180
give off some heat so you'll see some steam coming up off of the pile.

309
00:20:29,020 --> 00:20:33,900
A bulldozer is also used to push the aged material into a hopper that feeds a conveyor

310
00:20:33,901 --> 00:20:38,900
From here, it's a quick ride up to a shaker screen

311
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:40,000
that sizes the material.

312
00:20:42,700 --> 00:20:44,540
The pieces that are small enough fall

313
00:20:44,640 --> 00:20:48,220
through holes in the screen, while the oversized pieces,

314
00:20:48,320 --> 00:20:49,840
which fall off the end of the screen,

315
00:20:49,940 --> 00:20:51,720
are ground down to the right size

316
00:20:51,820 --> 00:20:53,420
and added back to the process.

317
00:20:55,660 --> 00:20:59,920
Next, the still damp material is fed into a dryer.

318
00:21:00,020 --> 00:21:02,020
And what this is is very similar to what

319
00:21:02,021 --> 00:21:03,820
to what your clothes dryer might be at home.

320
00:21:03,920 --> 00:21:05,420
We're actually putting the wet material,

321
00:21:05,520 --> 00:21:09,320
the wet wood material is in feeding on this side of the dryer

322
00:21:09,420 --> 00:21:12,560
where it starts out at 60% moisture

323
00:21:12,660 --> 00:21:18,000
and ends up at the end of the process at 5% to 7% moisture.

324
00:21:18,100 --> 00:21:20,280
Then the dried material is fed into the top

325
00:21:20,380 --> 00:21:25,380
of a large kiln-like chamber called a retort.

326
00:21:25,480 --> 00:21:28,320
As the wood material travels from top to bottom,

327
00:21:28,420 --> 00:21:32,020
it's exposed to temperatures that surpass 1,400 degrees

328
00:21:32,021 --> 00:21:36,260
On each level, four slowly rotating rabble arms act

329
00:21:36,360 --> 00:21:39,620
as rakes to make sure the material burns evenly

330
00:21:39,720 --> 00:21:43,560
and to push it onto the next level.

331
00:21:43,660 --> 00:21:45,360
By the time it reaches the bottom,

332
00:21:45,460 --> 00:21:48,900
the wood has been converted into char.

333
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:52,040
The char is then mixed with coal, other ingredients,

334
00:21:52,140 --> 00:21:55,540
and binders to hold the mixture together.

335
00:21:55,640 --> 00:21:58,140
At this point, the material has the consistency

336
00:21:58,240 --> 00:22:02,020
of a moist potting soil.

337
00:22:02,021 --> 00:22:05,520
What is occurring here is the mixture, the char

338
00:22:05,521 --> 00:22:08,020
and the coal mixture that we're feeding together

339
00:22:08,021 --> 00:22:11,520
is actually feeding down in between these two press rolls.

340
00:22:11,521 --> 00:22:14,940
This facility makes over 3.8 billion briquettes a year

341
00:22:14,941 --> 00:22:15,940
through these presses.

342
00:22:19,980 --> 00:22:21,600
After the briquettes are pressed,

343
00:22:21,601 --> 00:22:23,800
they travel over another shaker that

344
00:22:23,801 --> 00:22:26,340
eliminates any that are broken.

345
00:22:26,341 --> 00:22:29,440
Then it's on to the final drying process.

346
00:22:29,441 --> 00:22:32,020
The two dryers that you see on either side of you

347
00:22:32,021 --> 00:22:34,020
stretch 120 feet long.

348
00:22:34,021 --> 00:22:36,260
A briquette starts at 26% moisture.

349
00:22:36,261 --> 00:22:39,360
It takes an hour and 40 minutes to go from one end of the dryer

350
00:22:39,361 --> 00:22:42,360
to the other end of the dryer, where it exits at 5% moisture.

351
00:22:45,560 --> 00:22:47,860
These briquettes that we produce can be packaged

352
00:22:47,861 --> 00:22:50,980
at the end of this time directly into a bag.

353
00:22:54,080 --> 00:22:58,380
At Kingsford Springfield plant, more than 110,000 tons

354
00:22:58,381 --> 00:23:01,520
of briquettes are bagged each year.

355
00:23:01,521 --> 00:23:05,240
The barbecue purist may see the briquette as a compromise to convenience.

356
00:23:05,980 --> 00:23:10,040
But an even greater compromise to even more convenience is gas.

357
00:23:11,400 --> 00:23:15,740
For the last few years, gas grill sales have outpaced charcoal grills.

358
00:23:16,740 --> 00:23:20,280
Fueled by a propane tank or a connection to a natural gas line,

359
00:23:21,500 --> 00:23:24,300
these grills are ready to cook in a very short time,

360
00:23:24,600 --> 00:23:27,220
and their temperatures can be controlled with the turn of a knob.

361
00:23:27,221 --> 00:23:31,220
Multiple gas burners allow backyard chefs to cook at

362
00:23:31,221 --> 00:23:34,220
different temperatures at the same time.

363
00:23:34,221 --> 00:23:37,220
To avoid the flare-ups that often plague gas grills,

364
00:23:37,221 --> 00:23:40,220
many create a more indirect heat with a layer of ceramic

365
00:23:40,221 --> 00:23:44,220
briquettes, or tiles, that separates the flames from the

366
00:23:44,221 --> 00:23:46,220
food.

367
00:23:46,221 --> 00:23:52,220
One technological step beyond gas burners are infrared elements.

368
00:23:52,221 --> 00:23:55,220
Thermal Engineering Corporation, or TEC, in Columbia,

369
00:23:55,221 --> 00:24:00,860
in Columbia, South Carolina, originally developed infrared for applications like paint drying.

370
00:24:02,380 --> 00:24:05,940
TEC first adapted infrared for cooking in the 1980s.

371
00:24:07,680 --> 00:24:11,640
Now they're manufacturing grills featuring a newly designed infrared emitter.

372
00:24:13,400 --> 00:24:18,260
Beneath the grill rack is a plate of tempered glass that lies over a perforated metal plate

373
00:24:18,261 --> 00:24:22,160
designed to emit a mixture of gas and air evenly over its surface.

374
00:24:22,161 --> 00:24:30,740
when ignited the glass plate blocks the flow of hot air so that the food isn't cooked by convection

375
00:24:30,741 --> 00:24:37,180
but rather molecular excitation infrared is a form of electromagnetic radiation

376
00:24:37,181 --> 00:24:44,320
with wavelengths longer than visible light but shorter than microwaves these apertures are heated

377
00:24:44,321 --> 00:24:48,320
up to about 1650, 1700 degrees.

378
00:24:48,321 --> 00:24:52,120
And then we use glass on top of this as a secondary emitter.

379
00:24:52,121 --> 00:24:56,020
The family of wavelengths coming directly off of this berm is shorter.

380
00:24:56,021 --> 00:24:59,220
And once they go through the glass or re-radiated by the glass,

381
00:24:59,221 --> 00:25:02,820
they become longer in the electromagnetic spectrum.

382
00:25:02,821 --> 00:25:09,420
And what our studies have shown is that if we use long wavelength radiation,

383
00:25:09,421 --> 00:25:13,820
We have less moisture loss and less shrinkage in the food.

384
00:25:15,060 --> 00:25:16,680
But when it comes to barbecue,

385
00:25:16,940 --> 00:25:20,480
whether cooking with high-tech infrared or low-tech colds,

386
00:25:20,920 --> 00:25:23,620
heat and meat are only half the story.

387
00:25:24,740 --> 00:25:28,360
For many, the other half is all about sauce, sauce,

388
00:25:28,920 --> 00:25:31,080
and more sauce.

389
00:25:34,720 --> 00:25:37,780
If laid end to end, the yearly output of briquettes

390
00:25:37,781 --> 00:25:39,880
from Kingsford Springfield, Oregon plant

391
00:25:39,881 --> 00:25:43,200
would circle the earth 4.2 times.

392
00:25:44,720 --> 00:25:47,760
Barbecue Tech will return on Modern Marvels

393
00:25:47,761 --> 00:25:49,700
here on the History Channel.

394
00:25:54,060 --> 00:25:56,080
Barbecue is about flavors,

395
00:25:56,440 --> 00:25:58,660
adding mouth-watering and savory tastes,

396
00:25:59,140 --> 00:26:01,940
using a rub as the hands-on way to spice things up.

397
00:26:02,460 --> 00:26:06,060
But the wet and sometimes wild way most of us do it

398
00:26:06,061 --> 00:26:08,320
is with a generous slathering of sauce.

399
00:26:09,240 --> 00:26:12,120
The majority of sauces are sweet and tomato-based.

400
00:26:13,080 --> 00:26:15,940
But in the Carolinas, mustard and vinegar

401
00:26:15,941 --> 00:26:16,980
are the key ingredients.

402
00:26:18,460 --> 00:26:21,100
The Southwestern approach is to turn up the heat

403
00:26:21,101 --> 00:26:23,640
with chili powder and peppers that range

404
00:26:23,641 --> 00:26:27,220
from searing jalapenos to hellishly hot habaneros.

405
00:26:28,360 --> 00:26:31,180
Well, over the years, they've added a vast array of sauces.

406
00:26:31,620 --> 00:26:33,820
There's many different types of raspberry sauces,

407
00:26:33,821 --> 00:26:43,180
chipotle sauces, vinegar-based sauces, leading to the names of Whoop Ass, Gates, John Henry's.

408
00:26:44,300 --> 00:26:50,380
John Henry's first hit the shelves in 1996, and despite its growing nationwide distribution,

409
00:26:50,381 --> 00:26:53,340
it's still a small family-run operation in Houston, Texas.

410
00:26:54,460 --> 00:26:57,890
We started out with one gallon of barbecue sauce, with one customer.

411
00:26:57,891 --> 00:26:59,100
We took care of that one

412
00:26:59,101 --> 00:27:03,100
customer, make sure that one customer was always happy.

413
00:27:03,101 --> 00:27:07,800
We're around now, oh, gosh, 1,000 gallons of barbecue sauce,

414
00:27:07,801 --> 00:27:09,240
you know, a year.

415
00:27:09,241 --> 00:27:12,240
John Henry's sauces are made in a cramped kitchen,

416
00:27:12,241 --> 00:27:17,120
40 gallons at a time in a double-jacketed steam kettle.

417
00:27:17,121 --> 00:27:19,760
The main advantage of using this piece of equipment

418
00:27:19,761 --> 00:27:24,260
is that it protects its contents from scorching.

419
00:27:24,261 --> 00:27:27,300
A gas flame is used to heat water enclosed in a chamber

420
00:27:27,301 --> 00:27:31,200
the kettle, which makes steam that circulates in the space

421
00:27:31,300 --> 00:27:34,200
between the kettle and the outer jacket,

422
00:27:34,300 --> 00:27:38,940
creating an even heat, perfect for cooking up a sauce.

423
00:27:39,040 --> 00:27:42,340
To begin one of our sauces, we start with, of course, water.

424
00:27:42,440 --> 00:27:44,520
And then we start with the base of the sauce,

425
00:27:44,620 --> 00:27:46,620
which is called a slurry.

426
00:27:46,720 --> 00:27:51,000
And that is to give the sauce flavor, give it fullness,

427
00:27:51,100 --> 00:27:54,560
and to add more texture to the sauce.

428
00:27:54,561 --> 00:27:59,500
And ours has a perfect balance of sweet and spices.

429
00:27:59,501 --> 00:28:03,820
And there's some spices going in now.

430
00:28:04,660 --> 00:28:06,300
And my goodness, that looks good.

431
00:28:06,340 --> 00:28:07,040
You want to look at that?

432
00:28:07,300 --> 00:28:09,040
Just take a shot at that, will you?

433
00:28:09,980 --> 00:28:12,420
Yeah, that's what it started looking like now.

434
00:28:12,960 --> 00:28:15,220
This is a concoction of three ingredients

435
00:28:15,221 --> 00:28:18,980
that we put in to balance out the sugar.

436
00:28:19,340 --> 00:28:21,120
And you want to make sure that the pH is right.

437
00:28:21,121 --> 00:28:30,780
And then once you figure out what that pH, what the acid and the sugar balance is, that's the whole secret right there.

438
00:28:31,640 --> 00:28:37,980
Don't count on John Henry to disclose his ingredients, which are inspired by a love of barbecue passed down for generations.

439
00:28:39,420 --> 00:28:41,840
But he is willing to share some of his process.

440
00:28:43,600 --> 00:28:45,680
This is a beater or an agitator.

441
00:28:45,681 --> 00:28:51,320
And what you want to do here, you want to incorporate the slurry and all the other ingredients,

442
00:28:51,420 --> 00:28:56,180
the spices, into the tomato product that we just put in.

443
00:28:56,460 --> 00:29:02,180
So what we want to do, we want to go all the way down to the bottom.

444
00:29:03,480 --> 00:29:07,580
And once you go down to the bottom, you can see how thick it is.

445
00:29:07,600 --> 00:29:08,640
It's already getting thick.

446
00:29:09,360 --> 00:29:14,160
After all the ingredients are incorporated in the sauce, and it's cooked for an average of two hours,

447
00:29:14,161 --> 00:29:16,960
It's bottled in the unautomated assembly line,

448
00:29:17,060 --> 00:29:19,160
that same cramped kitchen.

449
00:29:19,260 --> 00:29:20,660
We started out with a mason jar

450
00:29:20,760 --> 00:29:23,060
because that's what we could afford back when we started.

451
00:29:23,160 --> 00:29:24,900
We never got away from this.

452
00:29:25,000 --> 00:29:27,600
People wanted to try to put it in a fancy jar,

453
00:29:27,700 --> 00:29:29,400
but we sort of balk at that.

454
00:29:29,500 --> 00:29:31,120
We wanted to stay with basic.

455
00:29:31,220 --> 00:29:33,220
Get through with the sauce, you can make this a water jar,

456
00:29:33,320 --> 00:29:34,220
you know?

457
00:29:37,020 --> 00:29:39,720
Original blonde in Kansas City, Kansas,

458
00:29:39,820 --> 00:29:43,520
also manufactures its own sauces.

459
00:29:43,521 --> 00:29:49,120
But as the supply of ingredients in their warehouse indicates, on a much larger scale,

460
00:29:49,700 --> 00:29:56,900
they also serve as a food processing plant for many other brands that don't want to invest the time and equipment to produce their product themselves.

461
00:29:58,100 --> 00:30:02,620
For our first-time customers, they come out here to the 80-gallon kettles.

462
00:30:03,140 --> 00:30:05,920
We work with them that day. They're in here with us.

463
00:30:05,921 --> 00:30:08,940
We perfect their sauce in the 80-gallon kettles.

464
00:30:08,941 --> 00:30:11,860
Once they determine that they like what we're doing,

465
00:30:12,300 --> 00:30:14,660
they're ready to move on to the bigger kettles.

466
00:30:14,900 --> 00:30:17,420
We bring them over to either a 200-gallon kettle

467
00:30:17,421 --> 00:30:20,020
or a 250-gallon kettle

468
00:30:20,021 --> 00:30:22,640
or the Big Daddy 300-gallon kettle.

469
00:30:23,180 --> 00:30:26,400
300-gallon kettle will produce about 2,200 units

470
00:30:26,401 --> 00:30:27,860
in a 16-ounce bottle.

471
00:30:28,880 --> 00:30:32,260
After original Juan's brewers mix the ingredients together

472
00:30:32,261 --> 00:30:33,580
for a particular sauce,

473
00:30:34,140 --> 00:30:35,780
they perform a thickness test.

474
00:30:36,620 --> 00:30:38,680
For this particular recipe,

475
00:30:38,681 --> 00:30:43,680
the customer wants to have a thickness of about 18.

476
00:30:43,681 --> 00:30:47,680
So I'm using a costometer that has a bubble in it,

477
00:30:47,681 --> 00:30:49,680
and you make it a level fill for the sauce.

478
00:30:49,681 --> 00:30:54,680
We're going to pour the sauce in.

479
00:30:54,681 --> 00:30:59,680
We're going to do about a 15-second count when we open the gate.

480
00:30:59,681 --> 00:31:02,680
Okay, right now we're at a 15 and a half,

481
00:31:02,681 --> 00:31:06,680
so that means I need to add a little water to it

482
00:31:06,681 --> 00:31:10,980
to get the sauce where the customer wants it in his bottle.

483
00:31:11,080 --> 00:31:14,320
Once the sauce makers achieve the desired thickness,

484
00:31:14,420 --> 00:31:16,760
they bring the temperature up to 190 degrees

485
00:31:16,860 --> 00:31:17,920
to sterilize the product.

486
00:31:20,760 --> 00:31:22,860
Then it's time to attach a hose to the kettle

487
00:31:22,960 --> 00:31:27,600
and pump the sauce over to the bottling assembly line.

488
00:31:27,700 --> 00:31:32,280
We fill our bottles hot at 190 to 195.

489
00:31:32,380 --> 00:31:34,380
We want to pull a vacuum to make sure

490
00:31:34,381 --> 00:31:36,420
that we get a seal on the jar.

491
00:31:36,520 --> 00:31:39,920
It runs down the line, then it gets a stamp date

492
00:31:40,020 --> 00:31:41,420
for a Best Buy date.

493
00:31:41,520 --> 00:31:45,120
Then it goes through a bander to put a safety seal on it.

494
00:31:45,220 --> 00:31:47,620
Goes through a heat tunnel to shrink that on there.

495
00:31:50,520 --> 00:31:53,040
Then it goes through the labeling system,

496
00:31:53,140 --> 00:31:56,640
into the box, and right out the door.

497
00:31:56,740 --> 00:31:58,840
Developing a recipe for a new sauce

498
00:31:58,940 --> 00:32:00,640
is one big challenge.

499
00:32:00,740 --> 00:32:02,680
Getting the public to buy it for the first time

500
00:32:02,681 --> 00:32:03,680
black is another.

501
00:32:03,681 --> 00:32:08,220
I have found that labeling is an interesting thing.

502
00:32:08,221 --> 00:32:14,000
You have to have a label that stands out with bright colors or a unique, something unique

503
00:32:14,001 --> 00:32:17,400
about it for people to pull it off the shelf.

504
00:32:17,401 --> 00:32:20,580
That's why we have come up with the Pain is Good line.

505
00:32:20,581 --> 00:32:24,060
It is an outstanding label to work with.

506
00:32:24,061 --> 00:32:30,640
Luckily for consumers, most sauces the pack of Wallop give fair warning.

507
00:32:30,641 --> 00:32:31,880
are sweetly seductive.

508
00:32:33,380 --> 00:32:36,140
But addictive new tastes aren't the only things

509
00:32:36,141 --> 00:32:37,280
tempting barbecue lovers.

510
00:32:38,500 --> 00:32:41,340
So are sparkling new toys to tinker with.

511
00:32:45,420 --> 00:32:48,840
The hotness of peppers is measured in Scoville units.

512
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:52,320
Jalapenos average 4,000 Scovilles,

513
00:32:52,321 --> 00:32:56,620
while habaneros can reach 350,000.

514
00:32:56,621 --> 00:33:01,620
Barbecue Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

515
00:33:03,620 --> 00:33:06,620
Barbecuing has gone beyond just outdoor cooking.

516
00:33:06,621 --> 00:33:11,620
It has become a lifestyle, and in some cases, an obsession.

517
00:33:11,621 --> 00:33:16,620
Top-of-the-line grills have become status symbols.

518
00:33:16,621 --> 00:33:19,620
Elaborate outdoor cooking islands can have all the conveniences

519
00:33:19,621 --> 00:33:24,620
of an indoor kitchen.

520
00:33:24,621 --> 00:33:29,780
Where the climate permits, outside has become far more comfortable than inside.

521
00:33:32,340 --> 00:33:33,740
Then there's the setting.

522
00:33:34,780 --> 00:33:35,480
Dr.

523
00:33:35,481 --> 00:33:39,260
David Biedebaugh has created a backyard that can serve up the pre-dinner entertainment,

524
00:33:40,300 --> 00:33:43,560
while the meal is prepared on an extravagant cooking island.

525
00:33:44,840 --> 00:33:47,340
When we moved in here, nothing was done in the backyard,

526
00:33:47,520 --> 00:33:50,720
and we wanted to put an outdoor kitchen in so that we could enjoy the view

527
00:33:50,721 --> 00:33:54,300
and be cozy by a fireplace and put a plasma TV in,

528
00:33:54,301 --> 00:33:55,960
and then a 24-foot barbecue.

529
00:33:56,600 --> 00:34:01,720
We put in Viking refrigerator, Viking ice maker, and a warming drawer.

530
00:34:01,800 --> 00:34:05,540
Now, this is important because you can put your plates in here until you're ready to serve.

531
00:34:06,280 --> 00:34:08,340
Then we've got a double-side burner.

532
00:34:08,820 --> 00:34:12,000
You can have a hot plate or you can create your sauces.

533
00:34:12,600 --> 00:34:14,760
You move over here into a 53-inch Viking grill,

534
00:34:15,060 --> 00:34:19,900
several burners, rotisserie with double infrared, as well as a true sear burner.

535
00:34:19,901 --> 00:34:22,800
Now, that has a low and a high getting up to 1,200 degrees,

536
00:34:22,801 --> 00:34:26,920
which sears in the juices very quickly, and you can move it over to cook slower.

537
00:34:27,180 --> 00:34:30,500
Or you can close the hood and make an oven out of it,

538
00:34:30,560 --> 00:34:33,480
take it up to 450, 600 degrees, whatever you like.

539
00:34:33,820 --> 00:34:38,980
Over here we've got the wok cooker, and it goes up to 32,500 BTUs,

540
00:34:39,060 --> 00:34:40,640
which is important for Asian food.

541
00:34:40,880 --> 00:34:42,200
This is a refreshment center.

542
00:34:42,500 --> 00:34:44,560
You put the ice in here, which you get from the ice maker,

543
00:34:45,020 --> 00:34:47,960
and you have your hard liquor, your condiments,

544
00:34:47,961 --> 00:34:53,460
and your ever-popular kegerator for a little party beer.

545
00:34:57,940 --> 00:35:00,360
Barbecue has become a gadget lover's paradise,

546
00:35:00,760 --> 00:35:03,860
and Dr. Bedebaugh is always on the lookout for something new.

547
00:35:04,300 --> 00:35:06,200
I like gadgets. I'm a consummate consumer.

548
00:35:06,440 --> 00:35:10,460
Here is tongs that for late-night cooking, I just push the button here,

549
00:35:10,560 --> 00:35:13,080
the LED light comes on, and I can see what I'm aiming at.

550
00:35:13,840 --> 00:35:14,720
It's beautiful.

551
00:35:14,721 --> 00:35:18,640
Barbecue stores are spreading like wildfire.

552
00:35:19,900 --> 00:35:22,360
Their shelves are crowded with specialized cooking aids

553
00:35:22,361 --> 00:35:26,620
and feature everything from tumble baskets to chicken thrones.

554
00:35:28,020 --> 00:35:31,780
Those eager to literally fill their meat with flavor can get to the point

555
00:35:31,781 --> 00:35:33,780
with syringe-like injectors.

556
00:35:35,500 --> 00:35:37,820
If you're cooking something bigger than a chicken,

557
00:35:38,400 --> 00:35:42,240
there's an injector to fill the bill, or in this case, the hog.

558
00:35:42,241 --> 00:35:43,740
And that's what we have here.

559
00:35:43,840 --> 00:35:47,280
We're going to inject the hog with a secret sauce.

560
00:35:47,380 --> 00:35:50,320
And what that does is kind of tenderizes the meat a little

561
00:35:50,420 --> 00:35:51,320
bit.

562
00:35:51,420 --> 00:35:53,960
We're going to inject it and let it sit for a couple hours

563
00:35:54,060 --> 00:35:56,960
before we actually put the heat to it.

564
00:35:57,060 --> 00:35:59,460
Meat thermometers monitor internal temperatures

565
00:35:59,560 --> 00:36:01,600
during cooking.

566
00:36:01,700 --> 00:36:04,760
This meat fork doubles as a digital thermometer.

567
00:36:04,860 --> 00:36:06,660
One of the tines is the heat sensor.

568
00:36:06,661 --> 00:36:11,660
sensor.

569
00:36:11,661 --> 00:36:15,660
Too busy to mind your meat? How about a digital thermometer that transmits a signal

570
00:36:15,661 --> 00:36:18,660
to a unit you can wear on your belt?

571
00:36:18,661 --> 00:36:21,660
Your food is ready.

572
00:36:21,661 --> 00:36:25,660
And if you're in the mood for barbecue but want to leave the work to others,

573
00:36:25,661 --> 00:36:29,660
all you need is a phone or a computer.

574
00:36:29,661 --> 00:36:34,660
After building a successful restaurant business, Jack Stack of Kansas City

575
00:36:34,661 --> 00:36:37,160
decided to get into the mail order barbecue business.

576
00:36:37,720 --> 00:36:40,620
My thought process when I decided on this shipping thing,

577
00:36:40,660 --> 00:36:42,520
it's kind of like, let's just get this going.

578
00:36:42,640 --> 00:36:44,460
Let's just find us a little space.

579
00:36:44,520 --> 00:36:46,380
And of course I found out that, you know,

580
00:36:46,420 --> 00:36:49,080
all the meat has to be cooked in federally inspected plants.

581
00:36:49,160 --> 00:36:50,400
They can't come out of your restaurant.

582
00:36:50,820 --> 00:36:52,960
And that was a curveball that we got through.

583
00:36:53,680 --> 00:36:56,520
Jack Stack found a USDA-certified facility

584
00:36:56,521 --> 00:36:59,280
where all the food preparation can be supervised

585
00:36:59,281 --> 00:37:00,860
by the restaurant's pit master.

586
00:37:00,861 --> 00:37:05,000
What we're doing today is cooking barbecue ribs.

587
00:37:07,340 --> 00:37:10,300
This oven here holds approximately 300 ribs at a time.

588
00:37:10,440 --> 00:37:16,480
It allows us to almost duplicate our open wood cookery that we use in our restaurants every day.

589
00:37:16,940 --> 00:37:20,620
By using this machine, we can control how much smoke we put on the product

590
00:37:20,621 --> 00:37:25,460
and also allows us to control the temperature of the product as we're cooking it.

591
00:37:26,300 --> 00:37:30,080
After the ribs have cooked for six to eight hours, they're ready to cool off.

592
00:37:30,860 --> 00:37:34,260
Once they go in the cooler, they're cooled down to about 38 degrees.

593
00:37:34,680 --> 00:37:38,580
From that point, we immediately pull them out and take them over to the vacuum machine

594
00:37:38,581 --> 00:37:42,800
where we vacuum pack them to seal in the freshness.

595
00:37:43,740 --> 00:37:49,620
The vacuum-sealed product is trucked across town to Jack Stack's cold storage and fulfillment facility.

596
00:37:50,820 --> 00:37:56,560
Renting space in an underground industrial park constructed in a former limestone mine has its advantages.

597
00:37:56,561 --> 00:37:59,560
It's temperature controlled year-round,

598
00:37:59,561 --> 00:38:01,560
security year-round.

599
00:38:01,561 --> 00:38:05,560
It's an ambiance of 63 degrees.

600
00:38:05,561 --> 00:38:07,560
It never changes.

601
00:38:07,561 --> 00:38:10,560
It makes it easier for our freezers to keep the product frozen

602
00:38:10,561 --> 00:38:12,560
at a certain temperature.

603
00:38:12,561 --> 00:38:16,560
When we're pulling the orders, the product stays colder longer.

604
00:38:16,561 --> 00:38:20,560
We'll put the ribs, beans, or whatever barbecue item

605
00:38:20,561 --> 00:38:23,560
the customer has ordered in a Styrofoam reusable container,

606
00:38:23,561 --> 00:38:28,480
container along with a cardboard box to keep it from being damaged through shipment.

607
00:38:29,340 --> 00:38:34,540
We'll then put a piece of thermal foil over the product. We'll put dry ice on there,

608
00:38:34,541 --> 00:38:37,028
which is 150 degrees below zero.

609
00:38:37,029 --> 00:38:42,160
It'll go to the customers through FedEx, and they get it and enjoy

610
00:38:42,161 --> 00:38:42,861
it.

611
00:38:42,862 --> 00:38:47,360
Mail-order barbecue can now make it to your table in about the same time it takes to slow

612
00:38:47,361 --> 00:38:52,300
cook a large cut of meat in true barbecue fashion.

613
00:38:52,301 --> 00:38:54,700
For one man, though, it's not just eating and cooking

614
00:38:54,701 --> 00:38:56,300
barbecue that's important.

615
00:38:56,301 --> 00:38:58,100
The art is in the pit.

616
00:38:58,101 --> 00:39:00,300
He's created some of the most outrageous ever.

617
00:39:03,240 --> 00:39:06,620
According to the Hearth Patio and Barbecue Association,

618
00:39:06,621 --> 00:39:09,620
81% of American households own a grill,

619
00:39:09,621 --> 00:39:13,880
and 65% of those grills are fueled by propane.

620
00:39:13,881 --> 00:39:16,220
Barbecue Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

621
00:39:22,000 --> 00:39:24,900
For those in the market for a truly unique barbecue,

622
00:39:25,500 --> 00:39:28,360
a visit to David Close's workshop in Houston, Texas

623
00:39:28,361 --> 00:39:32,400
shows why he's gained fame for his handcrafted creations.

624
00:39:35,400 --> 00:39:37,620
This is what we commonly refer to as bling-bling.

625
00:39:37,780 --> 00:39:38,740
The public named it.

626
00:39:39,100 --> 00:39:42,360
It's got 24-karat gold mags, gold handles.

627
00:39:42,361 --> 00:39:46,360
It also holds about 600 pounds of meat.

628
00:39:46,361 --> 00:39:50,360
Over here we have satellite TV, which gets 1,000 channels,

629
00:39:50,361 --> 00:39:52,360
and it's all solar powered.

630
00:39:52,361 --> 00:39:54,360
So it operates off of 2,000-amp cranking

631
00:39:54,361 --> 00:39:56,360
of staggered marine batteries.

632
00:39:56,361 --> 00:39:59,360
Now the paint job on this pit is a 100-year Ferrari

633
00:39:59,361 --> 00:40:02,360
metal flake burgundy with clear coat.

634
00:40:02,361 --> 00:40:04,360
What's interesting is when we put the clear coat on it,

635
00:40:04,361 --> 00:40:05,360
it candied it a little bit.

636
00:40:05,361 --> 00:40:08,360
Patent leather stools gives you something to sit on,

637
00:40:08,361 --> 00:40:11,360
and if you have a shelving, which is actually defenders,

638
00:40:11,361 --> 00:40:13,740
Then you've got gold-plated faucets and three sinks.

639
00:40:14,320 --> 00:40:17,580
You have 30-gallon water tanks underneath and holding tanks.

640
00:40:18,000 --> 00:40:19,420
Spare tire on the front.

641
00:40:19,960 --> 00:40:23,740
And then the gas injection for the firebox as well as the fish fryer burners.

642
00:40:23,741 --> 00:40:24,920
And you've got a complete package.

643
00:40:25,540 --> 00:40:29,680
David founded Barbecue Pits by Close in 1986.

644
00:40:30,680 --> 00:40:34,600
He manufactures several styles of professional and consumer pits,

645
00:40:34,820 --> 00:40:38,980
all built by hand in the spirit of old-style iron foundries.

646
00:40:38,981 --> 00:40:43,320
This is one of the composite pits that we built that's actually

647
00:40:43,321 --> 00:40:45,440
two separate pits built in the same line.

648
00:40:45,960 --> 00:40:47,740
It's probably about 12 feet long.

649
00:40:47,840 --> 00:40:52,340
It's got do-roll pull-out shells, slide-out trays, upright slow

650
00:40:52,341 --> 00:40:56,420
smoker, and a unique steak grill that hand cranks to any cooling

651
00:40:56,421 --> 00:40:57,360
height that you want.

652
00:40:57,620 --> 00:40:59,760
All you have to do is lock it and walk away, and it won't

653
00:40:59,761 --> 00:41:00,461
continue to cook.

654
00:41:00,462 --> 00:41:01,180
It'll keep it warm.

655
00:41:02,000 --> 00:41:04,520
David may have constructed some of the best barbecues

656
00:41:04,521 --> 00:41:07,920
ever built, and he certainly constructed some of the oddest.

657
00:41:08,980 --> 00:41:11,520
We have the distinction of building the world's largest pit in the world.

658
00:41:11,600 --> 00:41:14,580
It was 80 foot long and probably do about 15,000 pounds of meat.

659
00:41:14,920 --> 00:41:16,700
This is the world's smallest barbecue pit.

660
00:41:16,780 --> 00:41:20,724
It's fired with one pecan in the firebox and then just big enough to put one V.M.

661
00:41:20,725 --> 00:41:21,600
of sausage inside.

662
00:41:22,440 --> 00:41:24,860
I'm always looking for stuff to make into a new pit.

663
00:41:25,160 --> 00:41:26,360
We found this out in the country.

664
00:41:26,460 --> 00:41:30,580
It was crushed, so we straightened it out over a month period, and it turned out to be a baby carriage.

665
00:41:31,420 --> 00:41:34,540
Later on, we found out it was a 1906 baby carriage from London,

666
00:41:34,860 --> 00:41:37,840
so it shows that there's nothing safe from a welder's touch.

667
00:41:37,841 --> 00:41:41,540
Some of the things we made were foam booths, beer bottle pits.

668
00:41:41,980 --> 00:41:45,880
Just recently, a couple weeks ago, a friend of mine gave us a 1938 mailbox.

669
00:41:46,520 --> 00:41:47,600
It was completely rusted.

670
00:41:47,940 --> 00:41:51,420
It was amazing with a little bit of paint, add some shelving inside,

671
00:41:51,500 --> 00:41:53,200
and you've got a natural-blown Texas smoker.

672
00:41:54,780 --> 00:41:57,920
One of David's most meticulously constructed cookers

673
00:41:57,921 --> 00:42:00,820
was commissioned for the Continental Airlines barbecue team.

674
00:42:01,640 --> 00:42:06,720
It's an exact 1-10th-scale replica of a 777 wide-body jet.

675
00:42:07,840 --> 00:42:11,840
We went to David because he was the most renowned barbecue

676
00:42:11,841 --> 00:42:14,840
builder and pit builder in Houston.

677
00:42:14,841 --> 00:42:17,840
And sat down and started the initial drawings

678
00:42:17,841 --> 00:42:19,840
and everything with him.

679
00:42:19,841 --> 00:42:23,840
And as you can see, he did a wonderful job for it.

680
00:42:23,841 --> 00:42:26,840
What we find is people don't realize what it is,

681
00:42:26,841 --> 00:42:29,840
because we pull up and we've got an airplane

682
00:42:29,841 --> 00:42:32,840
inside of a trailer.

683
00:42:32,841 --> 00:42:35,840
And then we raise up the doors and people begin to realize

684
00:42:35,841 --> 00:42:39,080
begin to realize that it's a functional barbecue pit.

685
00:42:39,180 --> 00:42:41,180
And the doors are raised and lowered

686
00:42:41,280 --> 00:42:43,440
through a series of cables and pulleys.

687
00:42:46,180 --> 00:42:49,180
When we're cooking, we have the fire up to temperature.

688
00:42:49,280 --> 00:42:50,480
We close the doors.

689
00:42:50,580 --> 00:42:53,320
And the smoke travels through the fuselage

690
00:42:53,420 --> 00:42:57,660
and exits up through the tail on the top of the trailer.

691
00:42:57,760 --> 00:42:59,960
While David gets a kick out of taking barbecue

692
00:43:00,060 --> 00:43:04,240
into the 21st century, he's also a proud Texan.

693
00:43:04,241 --> 00:43:07,120
Rolling out his chuck wagon rig takes him back in history.

694
00:43:08,580 --> 00:43:10,760
Despite all the crazy contraptions we build,

695
00:43:10,900 --> 00:43:13,300
at the end of the day, we like to go out and take a model out

696
00:43:13,301 --> 00:43:14,820
and celebrate our Western heritage.

697
00:43:15,900 --> 00:43:19,320
We like to keep that spirit alive in the community of America.

698
00:43:21,080 --> 00:43:24,340
Barbecue really has become America's cuisine.

699
00:43:25,820 --> 00:43:27,780
Whether you spend way under $100

700
00:43:27,781 --> 00:43:31,620
or hundreds of thousands of dollars on equipment,

701
00:43:31,621 --> 00:43:35,620
It all comes down to a matter of taste.

702
00:43:35,621 --> 00:43:37,960
Barbecue is whatever you want it to be,

703
00:43:37,961 --> 00:43:39,820
whatever you want it to taste like.

704
00:43:39,821 --> 00:43:44,360
The two basic components that I would say will render out

705
00:43:44,361 --> 00:43:47,860
good barbecue is patience and have fun.

706
00:43:47,861 --> 00:43:50,380
Because if you're brushing it, you're not going to have the

707
00:43:50,381 --> 00:43:54,380
love that's going to emanate from the meat.

708
00:43:54,381 --> 00:43:57,220
After all, what word other than barbecue

709
00:43:57,221 --> 00:44:01,620
is not only a verb, but a noun for a word

710
00:44:01,621 --> 00:44:05,340
for a kind of cooking device, a cuisine,

711
00:44:05,341 --> 00:44:09,720
and an event that promises a good time, as well as good food.

